WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.620 --> 00:00:05.378
Why can electricity cost fifty euros in one zone, and ninety in the next?

2
00:00:05.408 --> 00:00:10.493
Power flows across borders, but the lines between zones have limited capacity.

3
00:00:10.523 --> 00:00:13.838
And when that capacity is scarce, it shapes prices.

4
00:00:14.453 --> 00:00:18.254
In this episode, we explore cross-border trading and market coupling.

5
00:00:20.520 --> 00:00:23.955
A transmission line can safely carry only so much power.

6
00:00:23.985 --> 00:00:28.906
Grid operators calculate how much of that capacity can be offered to the market.

7
00:00:28.936 --> 00:00:32.061
When trades would exceed it, the line is congested.

8
00:00:34.320 --> 00:00:37.159
There are two ways to allocate this capacity.

9
00:00:37.189 --> 00:00:41.241
With explicit auctions, traders first buy transmission capacity,

10
00:00:41.271 --> 00:00:43.600
and separately trade the electricity.

11
00:00:43.630 --> 00:00:47.426
The two steps can be mismatched, and capacity may go unused.

12
00:00:48.020 --> 00:00:51.485
With implicit allocation, also called market coupling,

13
00:00:51.515 --> 00:00:55.432
the capacity is used automatically inside the energy auction.

14
00:00:55.462 --> 00:00:58.862
Power flows from the cheap zone to the expensive one,

15
00:00:58.892 --> 00:01:01.063
and traders only deal with energy.

16
00:01:03.320 --> 00:01:06.298
Take two zones with simple supply curves.

17
00:01:06.328 --> 00:01:07.472
Zone X is cheap:

18
00:01:07.502 --> 00:01:10.187
on its own, its price is fifty euros.

19
00:01:10.217 --> 00:01:11.654
Zone Y is expensive:

20
00:01:11.684 --> 00:01:13.048
on its own, ninety.

21
00:01:13.653 --> 00:01:14.728
Now couple them.

22
00:01:14.758 --> 00:01:16.385
Power flows from X to Y.

23
00:01:16.415 --> 00:01:20.734
Exports raise the price in X, and imports lower the price in Y.

24
00:01:20.764 --> 00:01:25.774
With enough capacity, the flow reaches about twenty-seven megawatt-hours,

25
00:01:25.804 --> 00:01:29.157
and both zones settle at about sixty-three euros.

26
00:01:29.753 --> 00:01:32.796
If the line allows only fifteen, it is full.

27
00:01:32.826 --> 00:01:34.053
Prices stay apart:

28
00:01:34.083 --> 00:01:36.986
fifty-seven fifty in X, seventy-five in Y.

29
00:01:37.016 --> 00:01:41.665
The price difference times the flow, seventeen fifty times fifteen,

30
00:01:41.695 --> 00:01:46.065
gives a congestion income of two hundred sixty-two euros fifty.

31
00:01:48.320 --> 00:01:50.670
Real power does not follow contracts.

32
00:01:50.700 --> 00:01:54.273
It flows along all parallel paths, according to physics.

33
00:01:54.303 --> 00:01:56.653
In the Core region of central Europe,

34
00:01:56.683 --> 00:02:01.928
day-ahead coupling uses flow-based capacity calculation, which models these flows,

35
00:02:01.958 --> 00:02:04.437
instead of fixed limits on each border.

36
00:02:06.720 --> 00:02:09.375
In Southeast Europe, the picture is mixed.

37
00:02:09.405 --> 00:02:13.403
Some borders still allocate capacity through explicit auctions.

38
00:02:13.433 --> 00:02:18.646
Integrating Serbia into European day-ahead and intraday coupling is a stated goal,

39
00:02:18.676 --> 00:02:23.378
but timelines have shifted, so check the latest announcements from SEEPEX,

40
00:02:23.408 --> 00:02:25.232
EMS and the Energy Community.

41
00:02:27.490 --> 00:02:28.525
Three takeaways.

42
00:02:28.555 --> 00:02:28.792
One:

43
00:02:28.822 --> 00:02:32.453
scarce cross-border capacity creates price differences.

44
00:02:32.483 --> 00:02:32.719
Two:

45
00:02:32.749 --> 00:02:37.713
market coupling uses that capacity automatically inside the energy auction,

46
00:02:37.743 --> 00:02:40.975
sending power from low-price to high-price zones.

47
00:02:41.005 --> 00:02:41.374
Three:

48
00:02:41.404 --> 00:02:44.770
when a line is full, congestion keeps prices apart,

49
00:02:44.800 --> 00:02:48.431
and the difference signals the value of extra capacity.

50
00:02:49.023 --> 00:02:50.825
Thanks for watching Power Academy.
